}

// WHY THIS AUTHORITY EXISTS. `v2.workflow.floor_route_gap` gives an enrolled hermetic route gap a
// truthful DEBT disposition: the identity executed, reached a host effect the hermetic route has no
// arm for, and is counted and held. Held is not a verdict, and the changed-witness gate is right to
// block on it -- a witness that reached no verdict on the day it was authored has established
// nothing, and two enrolment rows added in the same change would otherwise let an author authorize
// their own witness's permanent non-execution.
//
// What was missing is not awareness of route gaps; it is an ALTERNATE TERMINAL to join against
// them. This module is that terminal: one receipt per scheduled identity, bound to the candidate it
// ran against, so "the hermetic route could not reach it" can be paired with "a route that can
// reach it did, on THIS tree, and it passed".
//
// THE RECEIPT IS BOUND TO A CANDIDATE, AND THAT BINDING IS THE WHOLE POINT. A terminal fact with no
// candidate is a claim that the witness passed SOMEWHERE, which is exactly the standing that let a
// deleted workflow read as coverage for months. The candidate is carried so a receipt from another
// tree is a typed refusal rather than an accepted substitute.

// COMPLETED-OVER-BUDGET IS ITS OWN ARM, NOT A NONTERMINAL ONE. It was folded into Nonterminal,
// which contradicted the producer: the claim REACHED its verdict and then the run exceeded a
// declared line. Interruption and completion send a reader to different remedies -- one to the
// route or the program, the other to the budget -- and the floor's own terminal authority already
// keeps them apart, so collapsing them here re-forked a distinction that exists upstream.
//
// It still refuses against this lane's only expectation. A member that passes only by running past
// its line is not a member this lane can carry; what changes is that the refusal says so.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE OWNS AFTER THE CONVERGENCE, AND WHAT IT NO LONGER DOES.
//
// This module used to carry its own verdict type, expectation type, scheduled row, terminal row,
// join, and seven refusal causes. `v2.workflow.floor_terminal_ledger` already owned the terminal
// vocabulary and `claim_disposition` already derived the completed-past-limit split, so a second
// spelling of both was a MEANING FORK: one fact answered by two authorities, which DESIGN section 3
// forbids and which had already begun to diverge -- this module's `LocalRepoWetRefused { detail:
// String }` collapsed five typed `ClaimOutcome` arms into prose, while the ledger types them.
//
// So the join moved to `v2.workflow.wet_evidence`, which answers it for BOTH routes, and what stays
// here is exactly what is ROUTE-OWNED and cannot be shared:
//
//   THE ROSTER -- which identities this lane claims, and the execution property that admits them.
//   THE REQUIREMENT -- that this route binds by CO-RESIDENT PREPARED SUBJECT and not by a
//   transported receipt digest, which is a fact about this route and not about the join.
//   THE INVOCATION WALL -- that an executor which was never called is a refusal rather than a
//   silence, which the join cannot see because it compares a schedule against terminals.
//
// The seven causes are preserved one-for-one by the shared refusal type and every one still refuses
// the same population: missing, duplicated, unscheduled, foreign entry, foreign function, and
// verdict-not-expected map directly, and the foreign CANDIDATE becomes
// `WetBindingPreparedSubjectDiffers` -- the same comparison, now typed by WHICH relation produced
// the binding rather than by an untyped String equality.

// THIS ROUTE'S REQUIREMENT, AND WHY IT IS A FUNCTION RATHER THAN A CONSTANT. The prepared subject is
// the tree the FLOOR prepared for this run, so it arrives as an argument; what is fixed here is the
// RELATION -- co-resident execution against that subject. A transported receipt cannot satisfy this
// route no matter what digest it carries, and after the convergence that is true BY CONSTRUCTION
// rather than by a comparison: the requirement determines the route, so there is no way to write a
// local schedule under a transported requirement.
fn local_repo_wet_requirement(candidate: String) -> WetEvidenceRequirement {
  LocalRepoCoResidentRequirement { prepared_subject: candidate }
}

// THE LANE'S COMPLETE ROSTER. Membership is by EXECUTION PROPERTY: every member's real effects are
// confined to a temporary directory and a local git repository, which the required lane can run in
// its own checkout. That is what makes std.witness_admission's LocalRepoWetLane route claim true for
// this population and false for BinWitnessWet, whose members also download ISOs, fetch over the
// network, build with real cargo and provision remote hosts.
//
// THE ROSTER DOES NOT LAND EMPTY, and that is deliberate rather than incidental. An empty roster
// would make the lane's coverage claim vacuously true and leave nothing proving the executor runs
// anything at all -- a gate permanently green by construction, which DESIGN section 4b calls a
// decoration rather than a wall.
//
// THIRTEEN MEMBERS IN THREE GROUPS, and the groups are named apart because they were admitted for
// different reasons and a single sentence covering all of them would be false about two.
//
//   SEVEN INHERITED DEBT MEMBERS. They were already on main, their effects are local-repo only, and
//   they had executed NOWHERE since falsifier.yml was deleted at 611fd02770 on 2026-08-15.
//   Scheduling them repaid standing debt rather than only serving the change that needed the lane.
//
//   FOUR CONTRACT-EPOCH MEMBERS, from the required-CI epoch work. They are NOT inherited debt: they
//   were authored with that change and have the same execution property, so they are admitted on
//   the same terms rather than on the same history. Each builds a real temporary repository and
//   commits into it, reaching shell.Mktemp.Dir, git plumbing over that worktree, and
//   shell.Remove.RecursiveForce -- no network, no cargo, no remote host.
//
//   TWO HOST-PROBE MEMBERS (added 2026-09-02, PR #10055, operator ruling relayed by tidy-lynx-804).
//   They are admitted on the NEGATIVE half of the property above -- no network, no cargo, no remote
//   host, no install media -- and NOT on its positive half: they build no temporary repository and
//   write nothing. Their only effect is a READ-ONLY HOST PATH PROBE (shell.PosixCommandV.Check,
//   command -v). That is stated as a distinct admitted effect rather than folded into the sentence
//   above, because a membership property widened to accommodate its newest member stops bounding
//   anything.
//
//   THE ADMISSION CONDITION WAS HOST-INDEPENDENCE OF THE VERDICT, AND IT WAS MEASURED. The risk the
//   ruling named is real: npm is provisioned by `apt install npm on falsifier cadence runner image`
//   (gunbc.host_cli_dependency codex_wet_materialization_host_cli_requirements), the cadence whose
//   workflow was deleted at 611fd02770, so npm cannot be assumed present on the hosts this lane
//   runs on. Both fns return the literal true from EVERY arm, and the npm fn was executed on one
//   host in both conditions -- probe found, and probe exit=127 with sh still resolvable -- yielding
//   the same verdict in each. A host-conditional red is therefore not what these import. The echo
//   fn's absent branch was NOT reached by execution (echo resolves in every PATH tested); its
//   host-independence rests on the same all-arms-true structure, which is total over hosts rather
//   than sampled.
//
//   WHAT THEY CAN STILL RED ON is route-unreachability, not tool presence: with no shell at all the
//   probe raises TypeError failed to execute 'sh' and reaches no verdict. That is the reachability
//   risk every member of this lane already carries, and it is recorded here so a future red is read
//   against the right axis.
//
//   FIVE EVALUATION-MODEL PROBE MEMBERS (added 2026-09-06, #10647). Their admitted effect is
//   stated here on its own rather than folded into either sentence above, because they satisfy
//   NEITHER exactly: they build no repository and reach no git plumbing, and unlike the host
//   probes they are not read-only. They write. The effect is a THROWAWAY TEMP DIRECTORY plus a
//   file inside it -- shell.Mktemp.Dir, Filesystem.Write, shell.Test.IsFile,
//   shell.Remove.RecursiveForce -- and each fn removes its own directory before returning. No
//   network, no cargo, no remote host, no install media, and nothing outside the directory it
//   created. Filesystem.Write is the new admission and is why this paragraph exists.
//
//   WHAT THEY MEASURE, and why ExpectedToHold is the honest expectation for all five rather than
//   the expectation that makes the lane green: they are effect-observing probes of the EVALUATION
//   MODEL. Two ask whether a `let` performs its bound effect when its value is never consumed;
//   three ask whether `&&` demands its right operand under a false left. Each answers by writing a
//   marker file, running the operation under test, and then asking whether the marker survived --
//   so the verdict rests on an observed effect and not on a reading of the source. The polarity
//   was taken FROM the measurement: `and_short_circuit_direct_probe` asserts the marker is GONE
//   after `false && shell.Remove.RecursiveForce(...)`, which is a claim that the right operand ran,
//   and it holds. Had the substrate short-circuited, that fn would be ExpectedToRefuse and the
//   disagreement among the five would itself have been the finding.
//
//   EIGHT MATERIALIZATION-STORE MEMBERS (added 2026-09-11, the provider-spine lane). Their admitted
//   effect is the evaluation-model probes' effect with one widening named here rather than folded
//   in: shell.Mktemp.DirWithTemplate (a /tmp/gunbc_matstore.* throwaway directory, because the
//   store grant admits only /tmp/gunbc_* scratch roots), Filesystem.Read, Filesystem.WriteCreateNew
//   and Filesystem.Write INSIDE that directory, shell.Test.IsFile, and shell.Remove.RecursiveForce
//   of the directory before returning. WriteCreateNew is the new admission: it stages, fsyncs and
//   hard-links within the same directory. No network, no cargo, no remote host, no install media,
//   nothing outside the directory the fn created. Each was executed wet through `gunbc run` over
//   this tree and held before the rows were written.
//
//   FIVE V4.1 PATCH-DIGEST MEMBERS (gunbc#11476). They reach sha256sum.Sha256.DigestFile over
//   committed patch paths in this checkout, or DigestStdin of the empty string. They write
//   nothing and create no temp directory. An unconditional hermetic mock_response was refused
//   (gunbc.hermetic_mock_fidelity). Sibling functions in the same entries stay hermetic.
//
// EACH WAS MEASURED WET BEFORE BEING SCHEDULED, not assumed runnable from its exclusion note. A
// member the executor cannot actually run would make this lane's route claim false, which is the
// failure the ruling that ordered this lane forbids by name. The four contract-epoch members were
// each run wet through `gunbc run` over this tree before this row was written: every one executed
// its real host effects to completion and returned true. That measurement is the reason they are
// here, and it is why an unmeasured member does not get added by analogy with a measured one.
//
// THE FIVE PROBE MEMBERS WERE MEASURED THE SAME WAY, on the same terms. The instrument is
// `claim_batch --wet --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry <entry> --functions <csv>`
// over this tree; re-derive the verdicts by running it rather than reading a number here. Two
// things about that run are worth recording because they are properties of the ROUTE and not of
// the members, and a future reader hitting them should not mistake either for a defect in the
// probes. First, the executor refuses at entry with `HostBudgetUnreadable` unless a cgroup
// memory limit binds the process; `GUNBC_BIND_MEMORY_CGROUP_BYTES` is the modeled request that
// supplies one, and the refusal is a missing binding rather than an unrunnable member. Second,
// the resolve reaches its own peak well above a default remote runner's whole memory, so a
// too-small host produces an OOM kill and not a verdict -- which is `SelectedButNotExecuted`,
// carrying no evidence in either direction.
//
// FIFTEEN FABRIC DB MEMBERS (test.claim.fabric.fabric_storage_file_store_wet_witness,
// test.claim.fabric.fabric_event_log_wet_witness and
// test.claim.fabric.fabric_partition_read_wet_witness). Their effects are a temp directory and files
// created inside it -- the fabric DB's object and head areas -- with no network and nothing outside
// the directory. They are the file store's refusal arms (lost race, missing object, a hole, a
// corrupt object, the bound, a navigating name), the served handler's store side, the read-only partition readout, and the event log's route over it. They replaced
// the three seat-ledger append members when the log moved off git (2026-09-19). Measured wet
// through `gunbc run` over this tree before scheduling: all fifteen held.
//
// THREE DEVBOOT TEXT-BLOB MEMBERS (test.claim.devboot_text_blob_real_execution), the same
// local-repo effects: a temp directory holding a bare store or a worktree repository, a symlink
// planted there, and git hash-object / cat-file inside it. Two are consumption controls for
// devboot's stdin route through both of its repository addresses; the third is the mechanism's red
// control (a path-routed hash of a clobbered path is git's empty blob), moved here from the retired
// seat-ledger append witness. Measured wet through `gunbc run` over this tree: all three held, and
// a writer mutated to drop trailing bytes turned the worktree member false.
//
// THE ROWS NOW CARRY `WitnessIdentity` RATHER THAN A QUALIFIED STRING, and that removed a duplicated
// fact rather than adding a field: every row's `identity` ended with "." plus its own `function`, so
// the qualified name was authored twice per row and could disagree with itself. It is derived from
// the pair by `witness_identity_qualified_name`, which is the same projection every other consumer
// of an identity already uses.
//
// CONTROL (a) and (b) -- two self-host behavioral receipts for dag/std/logic.dag, each verified
// by its own transport on the local-repo wet lane. Neither establishes the other; a green (b) does
// not cover a missing (a), nor vice versa. Joined by entry path rather than by proximity.
//   - CONTROL (b): test.claim.self_host_logic_seed_unavailable_witness /
//     self_host_logic_seed_unavailable_receipt_holds -- emit dag/std/logic.dag with gunbc,
//     compile the emitted Rust with standard infrastructure deps (im, serde, etc. for v1_rt.rs)
//     but WITHOUT the seed crate v1-compiler, run the witness binary. Proves the emitted logic
//     module executes without seed dependency at build/run time. The transport's v1_compiler:: call-site
//     check (per-subject spelling search) refuses if the emitted subject module references the seed
//     runtime namespace. Replaces the former zero-external-dependency constraint (now with standard
//     deps for v1_rt.rs, which the emitter includes unconditionally).
//   - CONTROL (a): test.claim.self_host_logic_behavioral_witness /
//     self_host_logic_behavioral_receipt_holds -- emit dag/std/logic.dag with gunbc, compile WITH
//     the seed crate (v1-compiler path dependency), run the comparison driver + --inject-fault.
//     Proves the comparison discriminates (DESIGN §5). Rehomed from FalsifierSelfHostWet (dead
//     cadence, falsifier.yml deleted at #8283). The DeclaredCadenceUnrealized standing for that
//     cadence is preserved in the exclusion row reason as evidence of the routing gap.
// MEMORY CAPTURE DIGEST MEMBER (2026-09-13, parent admission for gunbc#11144).
// This is a distinct admitted effect: execute the local coreutils `sha256sum`
// binary, supply captured text on stdin, and read its stdout and exit status.
// It writes no files, creates no repository, and uses no network, cargo,
// remote host or install media. It does not extend the command-v probe group.
// Measured through claim_batch --wet before enrollment: the production content
// verifier accepts the known-answer input and refuses changed bytes and an
// added terminal newline under the same expected digest. Missing executable or
// failed digest execution remains a refusal, never a fabricated hash match.
// TEN EFFECT-PLAN BASH MATERIALIZATION MEMBERS (2026-09-14, gunbc#11357; the If / Let / Call
// climb for gunbc-private#60). Their effect is executing a materialized script under bash through
// shell.Exec.Run: the commands inside are `id -u`, `test -x` / `test -f` over fixed paths, `printf`
// and `true`, run either directly or through a bound shell variable. They write no files, build
// no repository, and use no network, cargo, remote host or install media. They are on THIS lane
// rather than the dark bin-wet class because the floor plans the file's identities, each reaches a
// route gap on shell.Exec.Run (floor_route_gap chunk_04 and chunk_11), and a changed witness ending
// in a route gap is admitted only when a wet terminal joins it -- measured on gunbc#11357 run
// 34823848386, cause changed_witness_planned_without_terminal_verdict. The file's exclusion row
// (gunbc.ci.ci_layer_roots excl_local_repo_wet_bash_materialized_reason) records the move out of the
// bin-witness wet class, whose standing excl_bin_wet_reason carries. Measured wet through
// `gunbc run --claim-run` over this tree before enrollment: all ten held, and every metacharacter
// probe failed without output rather than running its payload.
// ONE ROW IS EXPECTED RED, AND THE REASON IS ON MAIN, NOT IN THE ROW. the_rendered_program_runs_and_its_
// output_parses_by_real_execution renders the census workload and runs it under a real shell; on the
// runners it observes failed on every main merge-queue run since #11731 landed it (the rendered
// program's `test -f .../gunbc-mtcollins1-census-...iso` finds no ISO in the ephemeral temp root),
// which the fail-open floor reported green over. Under the sealed floor (gunbc#11993) the schedule
// must say what is observed: ExpectedRed here, until the census-image lane gives the rendered program
// its fixture on the runner -- at which point this row reads ExpectedToHold again and the lane's
// schedule/terminal disagreement is the tell that flips it.
fn local_repo_wet_schedule() -> List<WetScheduledClaim> {
  [
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness", function: "effect_plan_bash_two_declared_operations_execute" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness_test.dag",
      function: "effect_plan_bash_two_declared_operations_execute",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness", function: "effect_plan_bash_fail_fast_prevents_later_operation_execution" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness_test.dag",
      function: "effect_plan_bash_fail_fast_prevents_later_operation_execution",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness", function: "effect_plan_bash_metachar_and_newline_cannot_open_a_statement" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness_test.dag",
      function: "effect_plan_bash_metachar_and_newline_cannot_open_a_statement",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness", function: "effect_plan_bash_let_binds_a_variable_a_later_operation_executes_through" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness_test.dag",
      function: "effect_plan_bash_let_binds_a_variable_a_later_operation_executes_through",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness", function: "effect_plan_bash_RED_let_value_with_metacharacters_stays_one_word_and_cannot_run" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness_test.dag",
      function: "effect_plan_bash_RED_let_value_with_metacharacters_stays_one_word_and_cannot_run",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness", function: "effect_plan_bash_call_runs_the_callee_with_each_arg_round_tripped_exactly" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness_test.dag",
      function: "effect_plan_bash_call_runs_the_callee_with_each_arg_round_tripped_exactly",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness", function: "effect_plan_bash_RED_call_callee_with_metacharacters_is_a_name_and_cannot_run" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness_test.dag",
      function: "effect_plan_bash_RED_call_callee_with_metacharacters_is_a_name_and_cannot_run",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness", function: "effect_plan_bash_if_selects_the_observed_branch" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness_test.dag",
      function: "effect_plan_bash_if_selects_the_observed_branch",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness", function: "effect_plan_bash_RED_if_operand_with_metacharacters_compares_as_one_word" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness_test.dag",
      function: "effect_plan_bash_RED_if_operand_with_metacharacters_compares_as_one_word",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness", function: "effect_plan_bash_if_branch_fail_fast_stops_the_branch_and_the_plan" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/effect_plan_bash_materialize_real_execution_witness_test.dag",
      function: "effect_plan_bash_if_branch_fail_fast_stops_the_branch_and_the_plan",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.memory_capture_real_execution_witness", function: "w_capture_digest_binds_consumed_content" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/host/memory_capture_real_execution_witness_test.dag",
      function: "w_capture_digest_binds_consumed_content",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.spark.spark_pair_head_unit_digest_witness", function: "hashing_the_same_rendered_bytes_is_stable" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/spark/spark_pair_head_unit_digest_witness_test.dag",
      function: "hashing_the_same_rendered_bytes_is_stable",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.spark.spark_pair_head_unit_digest_witness", function: "spark_pair_head_unit_declared_digest_hashes_the_realized_head_unit" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/spark/spark_pair_head_unit_digest_witness_test.dag",
      function: "spark_pair_head_unit_declared_digest_hashes_the_realized_head_unit",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.spark.spark_pair_head_unit_digest_witness", function: "unit_text_differing_by_one_byte_gives_a_different_digest" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/spark/spark_pair_head_unit_digest_witness_test.dag",
      function: "unit_text_differing_by_one_byte_gives_a_different_digest",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.serving.serving_availability_bind_wet_witness", function: "a_ready_route_reaches_the_existing_incarnation_frontier_in_the_product_path" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/serving/serving_availability_bind_wet_witness_test.dag",
      function: "a_ready_route_reaches_the_existing_incarnation_frontier_in_the_product_path",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.serving.serving_availability_bind_wet_witness", function: "a_withdrawn_roster_is_refused_at_the_declared_unit_not_the_incarnation" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/serving/serving_availability_bind_wet_witness_test.dag",
      function: "a_withdrawn_roster_is_refused_at_the_declared_unit_not_the_incarnation",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.serving.serving_availability_bind_wet_witness", function: "the_production_roster_carries_the_declared_digest_to_the_incarnation_frontier" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/serving/serving_availability_bind_wet_witness_test.dag",
      function: "the_production_roster_carries_the_declared_digest_to_the_incarnation_frontier",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.spark.pair_incumbent_identity_wet_witness", function: "the_production_declaration_keys_group_a_and_refuses_group_b" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/spark/pair_incumbent_identity_wet_witness_test.dag",
      function: "the_production_declaration_keys_group_a_and_refuses_group_b",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.spark.spark_serving_offer_route_witness_test", function: "an_assembled_looking_arm_with_adrift_ranks_refuses" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/spark/spark_serving_offer_route_witness_test.dag",
      function: "an_assembled_looking_arm_with_adrift_ranks_refuses",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
    },
    WetScheduledClaim {
      identity: WitnessIdentity { module_path: "test.claim.spark.spark_serving_offer_route_witness_test", function: "a_roster_that_could_not_be_read_refuses" },
      entry: "dag/test/claim/spark/spark_serving_offer_route_witness_test.dag",
      function: "a_roster_that_could_not_be_read_refuses",
      expectation: ExpectedToHold {}
